<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibadism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ibadi Islam, Ibadis, Ibadiyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibadism is a branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> distinct from both Sunnism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shiism</a>, which arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in the late 7th century and is named after the early figure Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn Ibad. It holds that the community should elect a just <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, who may be deposed if he breaks the law. Ibadis are the majority of Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, where Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamates</a> ruled at intervals from 751, and communities survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and Zanzibar.</p></div>
